== 4. every system call, and every one of its errors ==
  OK    the kernel shut down on its own (exit 21)
   -- open, write, read, close
  OK    open gave the lowest free descriptor (open = 3)
  OK    write wrote every octet it was given (write = 11)
  OK    close said nothing went wrong (close = 0)
  OK    read gave back exactly what was written (read = 11)
  OK    a read at the end of the file is 0, not an error (read again = 0)
  OK    and the octets are the same ones (same = 1)
   -- lseek, stat, fstat
  OK    lseek SEEK_SET answers with the new position (seek = 4)
  OK    and the next octet read is really the fifth one (seek char = 101)
  OK    lseek SEEK_CUR counts from where it stood (seek cur = 7)
  OK    lseek SEEK_END lands on the size of the file (seek end = 11)
  OK    fstat knows the size behind an open descriptor (fstat size = 11)
  OK    stat knows the size behind a path (stat size = 11)
  OK    st_mode says S_IFREG for a file (stat mode = 1)
  OK    and S_IFDIR for a directory (stat dir = 1)
   -- mkdir, getdents64
  OK    mkdir made the directory (mkdir = 0)
  OK    the second time it is -EEXIST (mkdir twice = -17)
  OK    the new directory shows up in the listing (found dir = 1)
  OK    entries the root directory has: 6
  OK    of them directories: 4
  OK    a buffer too short for one entry is -EINVAL (dents short = -22)
   -- dup, dup2
  OK    dup gave the next free descriptor (dup = 4)
  OK    the two descriptors share ONE position (the open file table) (dup positions = 4)
  OK    the original still reads after the copy was closed (dup2 = 1)
  OK    and a closed descriptor is -EBADF (dup2 close = -9)
   -- brk, mmap, munmap and the allocator on top of them
  OK    brk(0) says where the program break stands (brk = 1)
  OK    the allocator handed out a block (alloc = 1)
  OK    and what is written into it stays there (heap holds = 1)
  OK    the break really moved for it (brk grew = 1)
  OK    a released block is used again (the free list is one) (alloc reuse = 1)
  OK    mmap gave anonymous pages (mmap = 1)
  OK    the second page of them is really mapped (mmap holds = 1)
  OK    munmap gave them back (munmap = 0)
  OK    without MAP_ANONYMOUS there is nothing to map: -ENODEV (mmap bad = -19)
   -- pipe
  OK    pipe made a pair of descriptors (pipe = 0)
  OK    the writing end took the octets (pipe wrote = 5)
  OK    the reading end gave them back (pipe read = 5)
  OK    unchanged (pipe same = 1)
  OK    a pipe cannot be seeked: -ESPIPE (pipe seek = -29)
  OK    with the last writer gone the reader sees the end (pipe end = 0)
   -- fork, execve, wait4
  OK    getpid answers with a pid of its own (pid = 1)
  OK    getppid with a different one (ppid = 1)
  OK    fork gave the parent the pid of the child: 5
  OK    the child wrote through the pipe it inherited (fork pipe text = 4)
  OK    and what it wrote is what the PARENT had in its memory (fork sees = 1)
  OK    wait4 delivered the exit code of the child (fork code = 217)
  OK    fork + execve + wait4 ran a program off the disk (40 + argc, out of /bin/hello) (run = 42)
  OK    waiting for a foreign pid is -ECHILD (no child = -10)
   -- and every way to be wrong
  OK    a file that is not there: -ENOENT (err noent = -2)
  OK    a descriptor that is not open: -EBADF (err badfd = -9)
  OK    a pointer into the kernel: -EFAULT (err fault = -14)
  OK    a null pointer: -EFAULT (err nil = -14)
  OK    a call number nobody has: -ENOSYS (err nosys = -38)
  OK    reading a directory as a file: -EISDIR (err isdir = -21)
  OK    opening a file as a directory: -ENOTDIR (err notdir = -20)
  OK    opening a directory for writing: -EISDIR (err wrdir = -21)
  OK    unlinking a directory: -EISDIR (err unldir = -21)
  OK    seeking before the start of a file: -EINVAL (err seekneg = -22)
  OK    closing the same descriptor twice: -EBADF (err closed = -9)
